# What is NOT tracked under doc_chinese/
#
# The documents ARE tracked (see the note in the root .gitignore): the design document, the plans,
# the handoff memos, the audit reports and the bench scripts that produce the evidence they quote.
# Everything below is WORKING MATERIAL: large, regenerable, and only meaningful on the machine that
# produced it. Keep it out of the history.
#
# The rule of thumb: if a file is something a session wrote to run a measurement (a gnb console log,
# an IQ capture, a gate's dumps, a metallib snapshot, a tarball of the tree), it belongs here; if it
# is something a human reads to understand the port, it is tracked.

# Bench logs and run output (tens of MB each).
*.log
*.stderr
*.stdout
*/logs/
**/logs/
*/air_logs/
*/air_logs_*/
**/air_logs/
**/air_logs_*/

# Scratch trees: corpora, gate dumps, reference binaries, scratch tables.
work_tmp/
**/work_tmp/
*/gates/
*/ref/
*/corpus/

# Worktree backups (patches + copies of the tracked files at a point in time).
worktree_backups/
**/worktree_backups/

# Binary captures and compiled shader snapshots.
*.bin
*.metallib
*.tar.gz
*.tgz

# Nothing above 2 MB is a document: the largest tracked file here is a design document of ~1 MB.
# (Git cannot express a size rule; this block is the manual equivalent - see the table in
# doc_chinese/README.md for what is expected to be tracked.)
